# requirements/stellar/isochrones.ears
capability: MIST isochrone fits and stellar masses
owner: stellar
requirements:
  - id: STL-033
    pattern: ubiquitous
    text: "The stellar module shall read MIST isochrone tables as published, keeping each file's MIST version, MESA revision, photometric system, composition and extinction, and refuse a file whose layout, extinction, metallicity, photometric system or columns differ from what the settings expect."
    rationale: "MIST (Dotter 2016; Choi et al. 2016) is computed with MESA and published as .iso.cmd files: a header block of global values, then one column table per age. SDP keeps the provenance with the numbers, as MESA's own output files do, and applies extinction itself, so it takes only A_V = 0 files."
    verification: test
    tiers: [T1]
    status: approved
    traces:
      - "python/tests.stellar.test_isochrones::test_a_mist_file_is_read_with_its_header_values_and_every_table"
      - "python/tests.stellar.test_isochrones::test_a_file_that_breaks_the_layout_is_refused_with_the_line"
      - "python/tests.stellar.test_isochrones::test_the_grid_keeps_the_settings_columns_in_the_age_range_and_refuses_reddened_or_mixed_files"
      - "python/tests.stellar.test_isochrones::test_the_grid_survives_a_round_trip_through_its_stored_table"
      - "python/tests.stellar.test_isochrones::test_the_real_mist_v12_header_and_its_gaia_edr3_columns_are_read"
  - id: STL-034
    pattern: ubiquitous
    text: "The stellar module shall fit a cluster's members with the MIST isochrone, extinction and distance modulus that minimise their capped error-weighted distances in the Gaia colour-magnitude plane plus a parallax prior, and report each parameter's grid profile range and the share of members on the isochrone."
    rationale: "A real cluster is one age, composition and distance, so its members lie on one isochrone. The cap keeps binaries and interlopers from pulling the fit; the parallax prior uses the Gaia zero point (Lindegren et al. 2021). The profile range is the grid values within profile_delta of the best cost, not a calibrated confidence interval."
    verification: test
    tiers: [T1]
    status: approved
    traces:
      - "python/tests.stellar.test_isochrones::test_extinction_errors_and_the_parallax_prior_follow_the_settings"
      - "python/tests.stellar.test_isochrones::test_a_synthetic_cluster_gets_back_its_age_extinction_and_distance"
      - "python/tests.stellar.test_isochrones::test_an_old_cluster_is_told_from_a_young_one_by_its_turnoff"
      - "python/tests.stellar.test_isochrones::test_the_nearest_point_is_exact_in_each_stars_own_errors"
      - "python/tests.stellar.test_isochrones::test_too_few_members_or_no_metallicity_in_range_give_no_fit"
      - "python/tests.stellar.test_isochrones::test_a_profile_range_holds_every_value_within_delta_of_the_best"
      - "python/tests.stellar.test_isochrones::test_a_known_cluster_is_checked_against_its_window_by_any_of_its_names"
  - id: STL-035
    pattern: ubiquitous
    text: "The stellar module shall give each cluster member within mass_distance_max errors of its cluster's fitted isochrone the mass of the nearest point of it, give the heaviest living star's mass at the cluster's age, and give a field star the 16th, 50th and 84th percentiles of its mass and age over one metallicity's grid weighted by a Kroupa initial mass function and a prior uniform in age."
    rationale: "The dark-companion screen needs the visible star's mass; in a cluster the age is known, so the mass is fixed by the star's place on one isochrone, and no partner that is a normal cluster star can be heavier than the heaviest star still alive."
    verification: test
    tiers: [T1]
    status: approved
    traces:
      - "python/tests.stellar.test_isochrones::test_members_on_the_isochrone_get_their_masses_back"
      - "python/tests.stellar.test_isochrones::test_the_heaviest_living_star_bounds_any_normal_partner"
      - "python/tests.stellar.test_isochrones::test_the_imf_is_continuous_at_the_break_and_falls_with_mass"
      - "python/tests.stellar.test_isochrones::test_a_field_stars_mass_is_recovered_and_a_star_far_from_every_model_is_off_grid"
  - id: STL-036
    pattern: event
    text: "When `sdp isochrones fetch`, `sdp isochrones fit`, `sdp isochrones stars` or `sdp companions screen --isochrones` is invoked, the system shall write its results marked EXPLORATORY with the grid's MIST version, MESA revision and source SHA-256 and the settings' SHA-256 beside them."
    rationale: "Laid out as MESA lays out a run: a header of global values and provenance, one row per cluster, one table per cluster of its stars, and the same standard plots for every cluster. In a companion screen the isochrone masses sit beside Gaia's verdicts and do not change them."
    verification: test
    tiers: [T4]
    status: approved
    traces:
      - "python/tests.cli.test_cli_isochrones::test_fetch_reads_a_mist_tarball_and_records_its_provenance"
      - "python/tests.cli.test_cli_isochrones::test_fetch_downloads_the_settings_url_once_and_refuses_a_tarball_of_something_else"
      - "python/tests.cli.test_cli_isochrones::test_a_fit_writes_a_header_a_row_per_cluster_a_table_per_cluster_and_its_plots"
      - "python/tests.cli.test_cli_isochrones::test_a_fit_needs_exactly_one_source_of_clusters_and_the_stars_columns"
      - "python/tests.cli.test_cli_isochrones::test_field_stars_get_a_mass_and_an_age_range_each"
      - "python/tests.cli.test_cli_isochrones::test_a_grid_built_with_other_columns_is_refused_until_it_is_built_again"
      - "python/tests.cli.test_cli_companions::test_a_screen_with_isochrones_fits_each_cluster_and_needs_a_fetched_grid"
      - "python/tests.stellar.test_isochrones::test_a_screen_with_a_grid_adds_isochrone_masses_and_flags_a_partner_heavier_than_any_living_star"
  - id: STL-037
    pattern: ubiquitous
    text: "The stellar module shall give the same cluster fit whether its extinction and distance steps are matched in one query or one at a time, and whether clusters are fitted one at a time or side by side in separate processes."
    rationale: "The first PC run fitted 112 clusters in about five hours; most of it was start-up cost of thousands of small tree queries per cluster, and the PC has 24 cores idle. Speed must not change an answer."
    verification: test
    tiers: [T1, T4]
    status: approved
    traces:
      - "python/tests.stellar.test_isochrones::test_every_extinction_matched_in_one_query_costs_the_same_as_one_at_a_time"
      - "python/tests.cli.test_cli_isochrones::test_clusters_fitted_side_by_side_give_the_same_results_as_one_at_a_time"
  - id: STL-038
    pattern: ubiquitous
    text: "The stellar module shall name every fitted parameter whose best value lies on the edge of its search."
    rationale: "The first PC run put M5 and M3 on the old [Fe/H] limit of -1.0 and the grid's oldest age: an answer on the edge may lie beyond it, so a reader must be told."
    verification: test
    tiers: [T1]
    status: approved
    traces:
      - "python/tests.stellar.test_isochrones::test_a_fit_that_ends_on_the_edge_of_its_search_says_so"
  - id: STL-039
    pattern: ubiquitous
    text: "The stellar module shall pass a famous cluster's fit only when its age lies in the cluster's age window and, where the window gives one, its [Fe/H] lies in the [Fe/H] window."
    rationale: "M30 fitted at 1.3 Gyr and [Fe/H] -0.75 against about 13 Gyr and -2.3; globulars have well known metallicities, so their windows check [Fe/H] as well as age."
    verification: test
    tiers: [T1]
    status: approved
    traces:
      - "python/tests.stellar.test_isochrones::test_a_globular_is_checked_against_its_age_and_metallicity_windows"
  - id: STL-040
    pattern: ubiquitous
    text: "The stellar module shall give each cluster member its absolute G minus that of its cluster's main sequence at its intrinsic colour."
    rationale: "Many orbit candidates sat well above the isochrone and so had no MIST mass; how far above (up to 0.75 mag for an equal pair) says the companion shines, evidence against a dark partner."
    verification: test
    tiers: [T1]
    status: approved
    traces:
      - "python/tests.stellar.test_isochrones::test_a_star_above_the_line_has_a_negative_g_offset"
  - id: STL-041
    pattern: event
    text: "When `sdp companions screen --isochrones` is given a fits file from `sdp isochrones fit`, the system shall use each listed cluster's fit instead of fitting the cluster again."
    rationale: "The first PC run fitted the companion screen's clusters a second time after the sweep fit had fitted them."
    verification: test
    tiers: [T1, T4]
    status: approved
    traces:
      - "python/tests.stellar.test_isochrones::test_a_screen_given_an_earlier_fit_uses_it_instead_of_fitting_again"
      - "python/tests.cli.test_cli_companions::test_a_screen_with_isochrones_fits_each_cluster_and_needs_a_fetched_grid"
  - id: STL-042
    pattern: ubiquitous
    text: "The stellar module shall add to every fit's cost a Gaussian prior on [Fe/H] taken from the cluster's known spectroscopic [Fe/H] when the settings name it, from the Galactic disc's radial gradient when the cluster lies within z_max_kpc of the plane, and from the halo's distribution otherwise."
    rationale: "On the 2026-11-16 PC runs a Gaia CMD of 20 to 150 stars barely fixed metallicity: 43 of 112 fits sat at -2.5 or +0.5, young dusty clusters at -2.5, and the globulars (giants only, no turnoff) away from their known [Fe/H]. Catalogues of Gaia clusters use such priors; the prior is recorded with every fit."
    verification: test
    tiers: [T1]
    status: approved
    traces:
      - "python/tests.stellar.test_isochrones::test_a_metallicity_prior_comes_from_a_known_name_the_disc_or_the_halo"
      - "python/tests.stellar.test_isochrones::test_a_metallicity_prior_pulls_the_fit_and_is_recorded"
  - id: STL-043
    pattern: ubiquitous
    text: "The stellar module shall add red_dwarf_colour_error in quadrature to the colour error of every star whose absolute G is fainter than red_dwarf_abs_g, judging absolute G once per fit (at the parallax distance without dust, then again at each coarse search's best A_V and modulus until they stop changing, at most red_dwarf_passes coarse searches) so that every trial compared sees the same red dwarfs, and member flags use the same judgement."
    rationale: "The models' colours of K and M dwarfs are poor; on the PC a cluster of 19 red dwarfs fitted [Fe/H] +0.5 at a cost of 41, and with 0.15 mag more colour error it fitted +0.25 with 95 per cent of its stars on the line. Judged per trial instead (the 2026-11-16 22:40 run), a trial with less dust made more stars red dwarfs and won on their larger errors alone: 45 fits moved to lower A_V and higher [Fe/H], and fits at +0.5 went from 7 to 13."
    verification: test
    tiers: [T1]
    status: approved
    traces:
      - "python/tests.stellar.test_isochrones::test_red_dwarfs_carry_the_models_extra_colour_error"
      - "python/tests.stellar.test_isochrones::test_every_trial_of_a_fit_sees_the_same_red_dwarfs"
      - "python/tests.stellar.test_isochrones::test_a_dusty_clusters_red_dwarfs_are_judged_after_its_dust"
      - "python/tests.stellar.test_isochrones::test_member_flags_use_the_red_dwarfs_the_fit_judged"
